  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q=-6 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{2}+\\frac{5q}{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $2.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q=-6 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{2}+\\frac{5q}{2}=2 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q=-6 \\\\ 3p+5q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $p$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+4q & =-6 \\\\ 2p & =-4q-6 \\\\ p & =-2q-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $p=-2q-3$ into the second equation and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+5q & =4 \\\\ 3(-2q-3)+5q & =4 \\\\ -6q-9+5q & =4 \\\\ -q & =13 \\\\ q & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to find the value of $p,$ we substitute $q=-13$ into the equation $p=-2q-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =-2q-3 \\\\  & =-2(-13)-3 \\\\  & =26-3 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=23,\\,q=-13.$",
